<p>The danger of the Martingale system, or any progressive loss-chasing strategy, is rooted deeply in cognitive biases. The 'Gambler’s Fallacy' convinces a bettor that after four consecutive losses backing Ligue 1 home favorites, a win is 'due'. The mathematics of independent events dictates otherwise. A bankroll must be insulated from these human flaws through rigid, pre-defined rules that govern stake sizing regardless of recent outcomes.</p>
<p>Risk of ruin (RoR) can be approximated by the formula R = ((1 - edge) / (1 + edge)) ^ units. For a system with a 2% edge and a 50-unit bankroll, the risk of ruin is disconcertingly high. Increasing the bankroll to 200 units mathematically reduces this risk to near zero, assuming the edge remains constant. This is why professional syndicates operate with massive capital bases relative to their average stake, ensuring longevity over the grueling 38-game seasons of the top European leagues.</p>
