<h2>Why Humans Are Bad at Stake Sizing</h2>
<p>No edge can compensate for a flawed staking strategy. You might correctly identify a mispriced market in Serie A where the true probability of a draw is 35% but the implied odds suggest 30%. However, if you over-leverage your bankroll on this single event, a perfectly logical value bet can lead to catastrophic ruin. Professional capital operations demand that exposure is scaled mathematically, respecting the realities of variance and volatility inherent in a low-margin environment.</p>
<h2>Historical Data Analysis</h2>
<p>Psychological factors play a massive role in capital deployment. Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky’s Prospect Theory illustrates that humans experience the pain of a loss approximately 2.25 times more intensely than the joy of an equivalent gain. This loss aversion often leads to irrational stake sizing, particularly the temptation to chase losses. When a bettor faces a weekend of poor results in the Bundesliga, the mathematical response is to decrease stake size; the emotional human response is to increase it.</p>
